SUMMARY:Conference: Children\, Literature\, and the Christian Imagination
DESCRIPTION:Bringing together an international group of researchers from a range of disciplines and perspectives\, this conference will explore the intersections of children’s literature and the Christian imagination. Participants will investigate historical and contemporary expressions of Christian thought in literature for young audiences\, and reflect on the opportunities and challenges of scholarship in an age shaped by pluralism\, globalization\, and cultural polarization. \n\n\n\nThere is no fee but please register: https://stmikes.utoronto.ca/children-literature-and-the-christian-imagination
URL:https://stmikes.utoronto.ca/event/conference-children-literature-and-the-christian-imagination
LOCATION:Charbonnel Lounge\, Elmsley Hall (81 St. Mary Street)\, 81 St. Mary Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5S 1J4
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UofT St. Michael's College
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261023T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261023T200000
DTSTAMP:20260817T201138Z
CREATED:20260817T201132Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260817T201138Z
UID:10007551-1792778400-1792785600@stmikes.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Christianity and the Arts Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Frank Cottrell-Boyce is an internationally renowned screenwriter\, playwright\, and novelist and currently holds the position of Children’s Laureate in the UK. Frank Cottrell-Boyce is the writer of the award-winning London Olympics Opening Ceremony and of scripts and screenplays for well-known films and television programs (including Goodbye Christopher Robin\, Millions\, Framed\, The Railway Man\, and Doctor Who) and is a well-known collaborator of the theatre and film directors Michael Winterbottom and Danny Boyle. \n\n\n\nThe Lecture will be followed by a reception. \n\n\n\nThis event is free but please register: https://stmikes.utoronto.ca/children-literature-and-the-christian-imagination
URL:https://stmikes.utoronto.ca/event/christianity-and-the-arts-lecture
LOCATION:Alumni Hall\, Room 400\, 121 St. Joseph Street\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S1J4\, Canada
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UofT St. Michael's College
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261031T170000
DTSTAMP:20260804T162024Z
CREATED:20260804T155336Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260804T162024Z
UID:10007550-1793356200-1793466000@stmikes.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Magnifica Humanitas: Choosing Humanity in the AI Revolution
DESCRIPTION:— A Symposium on Putting Pope Leo’s “AI Encyclical” into Practice \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOctober 30-31\, 2026 (Fri/Sat)\, 10:30am – 5:00pm \n\n\n\nFather Madden Hall (in Carr Hall\, 100 St. Joseph St) \n\n\n\nFree admission / Lunch included: Register now \n\n\n\nHow do we maintain our humanity and agency in a world that increasingly revolves around AI? \n\n\n\nFrom workplaces to social media feeds\, from breakthroughs in healthcare research to that question you Googled last night\, AI seems to be permeating every aspect of our lives. But as the ways we think\, work\, live\, and act are being rapidly transformed\, how do we protect and nurture those parts of our humanity that matter most—our dignity\, our agency\, our creativity and moral commitments? \n\n\n\nJoin us as we discern our stakes in the current AI revolution through the lens of Magnifica Humanitas\, Pope Leo’s “AI encyclical.” Through two days of panels\, discussions\, and workshops\, we will explore how the Pope uses Catholic social teaching to engage with the structural realities of AI\, and consider our own practical responses to his call to prioritise human dignity over unrestricted AI development. \n\n\n\nBy asking practical questions about AI in light of Magnifica Humanitas\, this symposium will help us engage critically and faithfully with changing technologies in our own lives\, work\, and service to the world. Whether you are new to the conversation and wanting to learn more or are already confronting pressing issues around AI\, we welcome you to join us in this timely dialogue on navigating the emerging AI frontier. \n\n\n\n__________ \n\n\n\nMagnifica Humanitas Online Reading Group \n\n\n\nMonday evenings (7-8pm) from Sep. 14-Oct. 19 \n\n\n\nFree Registration Here \n\n\n\nAre you interested in Pope Leo’s “AI encyclical\,” but daunted by the thought of reading it alone? Have you been tempted to ask ChatGPT for a summary? Join us instead for this virtual reading group and explore the encyclical in conversation with others. Over 6 weekly sessions\, Dr. Ren Ito will facilitate discussions of each chapter of Magnifica Humanitas. Participants will build an understanding of the encyclical’s key arguments\, and will learn to engage with current issues of new technology through the lens of Catholic social teaching. \n\n\n\nParticipants will also have the opportunity to present reflections on and takeaways from the encyclical during a session of our October 30-31 Magnifica Humanitas symposium.
URL:https://stmikes.utoronto.ca/event/magnifica-humanitas-choosing-humanity-in-the-ai-revolution
LOCATION:Ontario
CATEGORIES:Continuing Education
END:VEVENT
